L1: Beetroot, watermelon and ginger juice supplementation may increase
L2: the clinical outcomes of Intracytoplasmic Sperm Injection cycles
L3: JBRA Assisted Reproduction (2023)
L4: Gabriela Halpern, Daniela Paes de Almeida Ferreira Braga, Christina Morishima, Amanda Souza Setti, Assumpto Iaconelli Setti Jr., Edson Borges Jr.
L5: 1  Fertility Medical Group, Av. Brigadeiro Luiz Antônio, 4545.
L6: São Paulo – SP, Brazil. Zip: 01401-002
L7: 2  Instituto Sapientiae – Centro de Estudos e Pesquisa em
L8: Reprodução Assistida Rua Vieira Maciel, 62. São Paulo – SP,
L9: Brazil. Zip: 04503-040
L10: Objective
L11: To prove the hypothesis that beetroot, watermelon and ginger juice
L12: supplementation improves the endometrial receptivity and clinical outcomes
L13: of intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) cycles.
L14: Methods
L15: This prospective randomized study enrolled 436 female patients undergoing
L16: ICSI cycles from January/2018 to June/2021, in a private
L17: university–affiliated IVF center. Female patients were randomized in a 1:3
L18: ratio to either Control (n=109) or Supplementation Group (n=327). All
L19: patients received nutritional orientation before the beginning of the
L20: treatment. Participants in the Supplementation Group were instructed to
L21: intake a daily dose of homemade juice, prepared with fresh beetroot,
L22: watermelon and ginger, from the day of embryo transfer until the day of
L23: pregnancy test, while patients in Control Group did not follow the juice
L24: protocol. Generalized Linear Models, adjusted for potential confounders
L25: (female age, body mass index - BMI, endometrial thickness upon embryo
L26: transfer, and number of transferred embryos), followed by Bonferroni post
L27: hoc test for the comparison of means between groups, were used to
L28: investigate the impact of juice supplementation on the clinical outcomes of
L29: ICSI.
L30: Results
L31: Patients and cycles characteristics were equally distributed among
L32: Supplementation and Control groups. Implantation rate (25.2% vs. 20.5%,
L33: p<0.001) and clinical pregnancy rate (41.0% vs. 22.0%, p=0.039) were
L34: significantly higher in the Supplementation compared to the Control
L35: group.
L36: Conclusions
L37: The use of beetroot, watermelon and ginger juice may be considered a
L38: promising strategy for improving clinical outcomes in assisted reproductive
L39: technology (ART), without any side effects.
L40: Original Article
L41: beetroot; watermelon; ginger; superfoods; pregnancy
L42: INTRODUCTION
L43: It is estimated that about 50 to 80 million people around the world experience
L44: infertility during their reproductive life ( Dyer
L45:  et al.,  2016 ). Since the first reported pregnancy
L46: following  in vitro  fertilization (IVF) ( Steptoe & Edwards, 1978 ), the use of assisted reproductive
L47: technology (ART) has increased dramatically worldwide and has made pregnancy
L48: possible for many infertile couples ( Sullivan
L49:  et al.,  2013 ). However, its efficacy in terms of
L50: take home baby is still low ( Sunkara  et
L51: al.,  2016 ).
L52: Many factors may influence the implantation of in vitro-produced embryos. Recently
L53: special attention has been given to patient’s lifestyle. There is a growing body of
L54: literature demonstrating that weight ( van Oers
L55:  et al.,  2016 ), diet ( Braga  et al.,  2012; 
L56:  2015; 
L57:  Best  et al.,  2017; 
L58:  Karay-iannis et al., 2018; 
L59:  Setti  et al.,  2018 ),
L60: exercise ( Ferreira  et al., 
L61: 2010; 
L62:  Palomba  et al.,  2014 ),
L63: smoking ( Borges  et al.,  2018; 
L64:  Budani  et al.,  2018; 
L65:  Mínguez-Alarcón  et
L66: al.,  2018 ), alcohol consumption ( Borges  et al.,  2018; 
L67:  Mínguez-Alarcón  et
L68: al.,  2018 ), and others significantly decreases the chance of
L69: clinical pregnancy.
L70: Different foods have biological activities that may harm or benefit health. In recent
L71: years there has been a growing interest in the biological activity of red beetroot
L72: ( Beta vulgaris rubra ) as a source of antioxidants and
L73: micronutrients including potassium, betaine, sodium, magnesium, vitamin C and
L74: perhaps most notably nitrate ( Clements  et
L75: al.,  2014 ), leading to increased nitric oxide (NO)
L76: availability ( Lundberg  et al., 
L77: 2008 ). Therefore, its intake has emerged as a strategy to prevent and
L78: manage pathologies associated with diminished NO bioavailability. Beetroot is also
L79: being considered as a promising therapeutic treatment in pathologies associated with
L80: oxidative stress and inflammation ( Clifford
L81:  et al.,  2015 ). Previous data demonstrate that a
L82: single dose of 140 mL of beetroot juice consumption improves vascular endothelial
L83: function ( Volino-Souza  et al., 
L84: 2018 ).
L85: It has also been suggested that increased l-arginine levels in the circulation may
L86: represent a potential therapeutic mechanism to increase NO synthesis and
L87: bioavailability. However, oral l-arginine supplementation is largely ineffective due
L88: to gastrointestinal and hepatic extraction of l-arginine ( Allerton  et al.,  2018 ). Alternatively, oral
L89: l-citrulline supplementation consistently increases plasma and tissue levels of
L90: l-arginine and NO bioavailability ( Moinard
L91:  et al.,  2008; 
L92:  Schwedhelm  et al.,  2008 ).
L93: I-Citrulline is a neutral, non-essential alpha-amino acid, rarely found in food, but
L94: highly concentrated in watermelon ( Citrullus lanatus ) ( Curis  et al.,  2005 ).
L95: In addition to the beetroot and watermelon, compounds found in ginger
L96: ( Zingiber officinale ), particularly the 6-Gingerol, has been
L97: reported to possess a variety of biological properties including anti-oxidant,
L98: anti-inflammatory, anti-platelet aggregation, antifungal, antimicrobial, and
L99: anticancer activities. Ginger is also known to decrease the activities of
L100: angiotensin-1 converting enzyme and arginase and increased the level of NO ( Mao  et al.,  2019 ).
L101: Embryo implantation depends on the presence of a viable embryo in a receptive
L102: endometrium. The endometrium is a highly dynamic tissue that undergoes cyclic
L103: cellular proliferation, differentiation, and immune cell trafficking in response to
L104: changing circulating ovarian-derived steroids ( Valdes  et al.,  2017 ). Endometrium angiogenesis play a
L105: crucial role during the menstrual cycle, particularly around the time of embryo
L106: implantation ( Gargett & Rogers, 2001 ).
L107: Any alteration in endometrial vascularization may lead to unsuccessful
L108: implantation.
L109: The goal for the present study was to evaluate weather beetroot, watermelon and
L110: ginger juice supplementation would improve the endometrial receptivity and clinical
L111: outcomes of intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) cycles.
L112: MATERIALS AND METHODS
L113: Study design
L114: This prospective randomized study enrolled 436 female patients undergoing ICSI
L115: cycles from January/2018 to June/2021, in a private university-affiliated IVF
L116: center. Female patients were randomized in a 1:3 ratio to either Control (n=109)
L117: or Supplementation Group (n=327). All patients received nutritional orientation
L118: before the beginning of the treatment. Participants in the Supplementation Group
L119: were instructed to intake a daily dose of homemade juice, prepared with fresh
L120: beetroot, watermelon and ginger, from the day of embryo transfer until the day
L121: of pregnancy test, while patients in Control Group did not follow the juice
L122: protocol. The impact of juice supplementation on the clinical outcomes of ICSI
L123: was investigated.
L124: Clinical pregnancy was defined as the presence of a gestational sac that could be
L125: visualized using ultrasound 4-6 weeks after embryo transfer, and miscarriage was
L126: defined as pregnancy with a total loss of gestational sacs before 20 weeks’
L127: gestation.
L128: Written informed consent was obtained, in which patients agreed to share the
L129: outcomes of their own cycles for research purposes, and the study was approved
L130: by the local institutional review board.
L131: Controlled ovarian stimulation and laboratory procedures
L132: Controlled ovarian stimulation was performed using recombinant
L133: follicle-stimulating hormone (r-FSH, Gonal-F®; Serono, Geneva,
L134: Switzerland), with pituitary blockage using a gonadotropin-releasing hormone
L135: (GnRH) antagonist, cetrorelix acetate (Cetrotide®; Merck KGaA, Serono,
L136: Geneva, Switzerland).
L137: Follicular growth was monitored using transvaginal ultrasound examination
L138: starting on day 4 of gonadotropin administration. When adequate follicular
L139: growth and serum estradiol levels were observed, leuprolide acetate
L140: (Lupron®; TAP Pharmaceuticals, North Chicago, IL, United States) was
L141: administered to trigger the final follicular maturation. The oocytes were
L142: collected 35 hours later through transvaginal ultrasound ovum pickup.
L143: The recovered oocytes were assessed to determine their nuclear status, and those
L144: in metaphase II were submitted to intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI)
L145: following routine procedures ( Palermo  et
L146: al.,  1997 ).
L147: After ICSI oocyte/embryos were cultured until day five when one or two
L148: blastocysts were transfer.
L149: Embryo morphology evaluation
L150: Embryo morphology was assessed: (i) 16 to 18 hours post-ICSI ± 1hour, (ii)
L151: 44 hours ± 1hour post-ICSI, for day two evaluation (iii) 68 hours
L152: ± 1hour post-ICSI, for day three evaluation (iv) 116 hours ± 2hour
L153: post-ICSI for day five evaluation and (v) 120 hour ± 2hour post-ICSI for
L154: late day five evaluation (transfer time). Embryo morphology was performed using
L155: an inverted Nikon Diaphot microscope (Eclipse TE 300; Nikon, Tokyo, Japan) with
L156: a Hoffmann modulation contrast system under 400X magnification and was considere
L157: to choose best embryos for transfer.
L158: Statistical analysis
L159: The sample size calculation suggested that 265 cycles at least would be enough to
L160: demonstrate a 20% effect with 90% power and 5% significance level considering as
L161: primary outcome clinical pregnancy rate. An increase of 33% in sample size was
L162: required due to a 3:1 randomization. Sample size calculation was performed using
L163: G*Power 3.1.7.
L164: Generalized Linear Models, adjusted for potential confounders (female age, FSH
L165: dose used for controlled ovarian stimulation, endometrial thickness upon embryo
L166: transfer, number of transferred embryos and high quality embryos rate), followed
L167: by Bonferroni post hoc test for the comparison of means between groups were used
L168: to investigate the impact of juice supplementation on the clinical outcomes of
L169: ICSI.
L170: Data are expressed as mean ± standard error for continuous variables or
L171: percentages for dichotomous variables, and  p -values.
L172:  P -value was significant at 5% level (<0.05). The
L173: analysis was performed using SPSS Statistics 21 (IBM, New York, New York,
L174: USA).
L175: RESULTS
L176: Patient and cycle characteristics
L177: Similar maternal and paternal ages, maternal BMI, estradiol level on hCG trigger
L178: day, FSH dose used for controlled ovarian stimulation, number of follicles,
L179: number of retrieved and mature oocytes, mature oocytes rate, fertilization rate,
L180: high quality embryo rate, blastocyst development rate, number of transferred
L181: embryos and endometrial thickness were observed between the groups ( Table 1 ).
L182: Table 1 Patient and cycle characteristics for the positive and negative pregnancy
L183: groups.
L184: Influence of juicy supplementation of ICSI outcomes
L185: Implantation rate and clinical pregnancy rate were significantly higher in the
L186: Supplementation compared to the Control group, while the miscarriage rate didn’t
L187: differ among the groups ( Table 2 ).
L188: Table 2 Generalized linear model results for the comparison of implantation,
L189: pregnancy and miscarriage rate between Supplementation and Control
L190: groups.
L191: DISCUSSION
L192: Infertility has considerably increased over the past decades ( Bushnik  et al.,  2012; 
L193:  Luke, 2017 ). Besides the trend among women in
L194: developed countries to delay motherhood ( Datta
L195:  et al.,  2016 ), lifestyles factors may be pointed as
L196: reasons for the impaired reproduction potential ( Harley  et al.,  2008; 
L197:  Boots & Stephenson, 2011; 
L198:  Braga  et al.,  2012; 
L199:  2015; 
L200:  Practice Committee of the American Society for
L201: Reproductive Medicine, 2012; 
L202:  Buck Louis, 2014 ). For the present study
L203: weather beetroot, watermelon and ginger juice supplementation would improve clinical
L204: outcomes of ICSI cycles, was evaluated. The results showed that both implantation
L205: and clinical pregnancy rate were significantly higher when the juice was taken
L206: compared to control group. Additionally a significant difference in miscarriage rate
L207: was noted between the groups.
L208: Subjects in the supplementation group were oriented to take a daily dose of the
L209: juice, from the day of embryo transfer until the day of pregnancy test, a critical
L210: period determining implantation of embryo in uterus. Molecular and genetic evidence
L211: indicates that ovarian hormones together with locally produced signaling molecules,
L212: function through autocrine, paracrine and juxtracrine interactions to specify the
L213: complex process of implantation ( Zhang  et
L214: al.,  2013 ). Growth factors, particularly the endothelial
L215: growth factor (VEGF), are involved in a fundamental process in embryo implantation,
L216: the angiogenesis ( Chen  et al., 
L217: 2017 ).
L218: It has been speculated that recurrent reproductive failure, including recurrent
L219: miscarriage ( Chen  et al., 
L220: 2016 ) and recurrent implantation failure ( Goodman  et al.,  2008 ) after IVF are two events where
L221: abnormal endometrial angiogenesis may occur.
L222: The cellular processes of angiogenesis involves hypoxia which induces the production
L223: of NO and the expression of VEGF and angiopoietin-1 and -2, which interact with
L224: extracellular matrix proteases to increase the permeability of the capillary vessel
L225: wall. Increased permeability leads to the escape of plasma proteins and fibrinogen
L226: from capillary vessels, promoting the formation of transient extracellular matrix,
L227: which supports the growth of new vessels ( de Castro
L228: Junior  et al.,  2006; 
L229:  Lin  et al.,  2016 ).
L230: The role of NO for the vascular health has been extensively studied. It acts as a
L231: hormone and its activities are generally beneficial. For example, it is a powerful
L232: generator of vasodilation by suppressing vascular smooth muscle contraction. Nitric
L233: oxide also inhibits platelet aggregation and the adhesion of leukocytes to
L234: endothelia ( Litwack, 2014 ). Consequently, NO
L235: becomes a substance of interest in therapeutic applications.
L236: The most recognized pathway for NO generation is the oxidation of L-arginine
L237: catalyzed by the NO synthase enzymes to yield NO and L-citrulline ( Moncada & Higgs, 1991 ). Although
L238: traditionally recognized as a product of the NO synthase-mediated oxidation of
L239: L-arginine, recent evidence suggests that L-citrulline can be recycled back to
L240: L-arginine ( Haines  et al., 
L241: 2011 ) and is more effective than orally ingested L-arginine ( Osowska  et al.,  2004; 
L242:  Schwedhelm  et al.,  2008 ). It
L243: has also been reported that L-citrulline supplementation can increase the activity
L244: of NO synthase ( Wijnands  et al., 
L245: 2012 ) and increase NO bio-markers ( Schwedhelm  et al.,  2008 ). Therefore, short-term
L246: supplementation with L-citrulline might represent an effective dietary intervention
L247: to increase NO synthase.
L248: Watermelon juice is a particularly rich source L-citrulline. It has been reported
L249: that watermelon juice contains ~2.33g of L-citrulline per L ( Tarazona-Díaz  et al.,  2013 ). Moreover,
L250: watermelon contains lycopene, a lipophilic, unsaturated carotenoid, also known to
L251: improve vascular health ( Gajendragadkar  et
L252: al.,  2014 ) by several different mechanisms ( Mozos  et al.,  2018 ).
L253: The watermelon is not the only food recognized to improve vascular heath, in recent
L254: years the beetroot has attracted much attention as a health promoting functional
L255: food. Interest in beetroot has been primarily driven by the discovery that sources
L256: of dietary nitrate may have important implications for managing cardiovascular
L257: health ( Lundberg  et al., 
L258: 2008 ). Beetroot is also rich in several other bioactive compounds that
L259: may provide health benefits, particularly for disorders characterized by chronic
L260: inflammation ( Clifford  et al., 
L261: 2015 ).
L262: In fact, in addition to the effect on vascular health, the intake of both beetroot
L263: and watermelon has important antioxidant effects. In line with this, another
L264: superfood, the ginger has been reported to possess multiple biological activities,
L265: including antioxidant and anti-inflammatory ( Mao
L266:  et al.,  2019 ).
L267: The use of this functional food has been widely studied as a source of energy during
L268: exercise ( Barlow  et al., 
L269: 2018; 
L270:  Ridwan  et al.,  2019 ) or for
L271: the prevention and management of chronic diseases ( Zhang  et al.,  2016 ), but not for reproductive health.
L272: Considering that implantation failure may be a consequence of a diminished
L273: endometrial receptivity due to, among other causes, a weak angiogenesis or excessive
L274: oxidative stress, we have hypothesized that the intake of foods such as beetroot,
L275: watermelon and ginger would improve endometrial vascularization, reduce oxidative
L276: stress and therefore improve embryo implantation in women undergoing ART.
L277: The intake of beetroot, watermelon and ginger juice significantly improved
L278: implantation and pregnancy rates. It could be argued that among so many variables
L279: influencing embryo implantation, the intake of some specific foods for such a short
L280: period of time could not be the reason for the improved clinical outcomes. However,
L281: NO action is rapid as it remains in the blood for only seconds and its short term
L282: effect in human have been already demonstrated ( Bailey  et al. , 2016; 
L283:  Porcelli  et al.,  2016 ).
L284: Moreover, previous evidence has suggested that there is an alteration of endometrial
L285: receptivity in patients with recurrent implantation failure. Women with repeated
L286: donor insemination failures have altered endometrial progression in relation to
L287: their menstrual cycle ( Li  et al., 
L288: 1993 ). Hysteroscopy studies have shown that 18%-50% of recurrent
L289: implantation failure patients have abnormalities of the uterine cavity ( Margalioth  et al.,  2006 ),
L290: while temporal displacement of the window of implantation has been described in one
L291: out of four repeated implantation failure patients ( Ruiz-Alonso  et al.,  2013 ). Therefore our evidence
L292: suggests that the intake of beetroot, watermelon and ginger juice from the embryo
L293: transfer day would have improved the endometrial angiogenesis and microenvironment
L294: by and increased NO, lycopene and antioxidants bioavailability favoring embryo
L295: implantation.
L296: CONCLUSIONS
L297: Recently, lifestyle behaviors such as dietary intake, has been pointed as the
L298: strongest influencing factors related to human health. Researchers have put a lot of
L299: effort into understanding the impact of the selective use of isolated nutrients and
L300: nutritional supplements to enhance human health and functional capacity. Our results
L301: demonstrated that the use of specific nutrients may also improve reproductive
L302: health. The use of beetroot, watermelon and ginger juice may be considered a
L303: promising strategy for improving clinical outcomes in ART, without any side
L304: effects.
